NAME
       ping - check that another machine answers on the network

SYNOPSIS
       ping [-c COUNT] [-i INTERVAL] [-W TIMEOUT] [-w DEADLINE] [-q] [-n]
            [-4 | -6] [-I INTERFACE] [-s SIZE] HOST

DESCRIPTION
       ping sends ICMP echo requests to HOST (a name or an address) and
       prints a line for every reply: its size, the sequence number, the TTL
       and the round-trip time. When it ends it prints a summary: packets
       sent and received, the loss in percent and the times.

       Without -c, ping goes on until you press Ctrl+C. In scripts and on the
       exam, always give -c.

       ping is the first test when the network does not work. Go outward step
       by step: your own address, the gateway, another machine by address,
       then by name. If addresses work but names do not, the problem is name
       resolution (/etc/hosts, the DNS servers in the connection profile), not
       the network.

       "Destination Host Unreachable" means nobody on the local network
       answered for that address (wrong address, wrong subnet, cable or
       interface down). "Name or service not known" means the name could not
       be resolved. A firewall may also drop pings; that does not prove the
       host is down.

       The exit status is 0 when at least one reply came back, 1 when none
       did and 2 on other errors, so ping -c 1 works in if-tests.

OPTIONS
       -c COUNT
              Stop after COUNT requests.

       -i SECONDS
              Wait this long between requests (default 1).

       -W SECONDS
              How long to wait for each reply.

       -w SECONDS
              Stop after this many seconds in total.

       -q     Quiet: print only the summary.

       -n     Print addresses only; do not look up names.

       -4, -6 Use IPv4 or IPv6 only.

       -I INTERFACE
              Send from this interface or source address.

       -s SIZE
              Send SIZE bytes of data (default 56).

EXAMPLES
       Check the gateway, then server2 by name:

           ping -c 3 192.168.50.254
           ping -c 3 server2.lab.example.com

       Test IPv6 on this machine (the loopback address):

           ping -6 -c 2 ::1

       In a script:

           if ping -c 1 -W 2 utility.lab.example.com > /dev/null; then
               echo "utility is up"
           fi

SEE ALSO
       ip(8), nmcli(1), tracepath(8), ss(8), host(1)
